# Build and physically certify the custom hardware

## Scope and existing evidence

Treat the device as a separate product with its own electronics, firmware,
enclosure, safety, calibration, endurance, and manufacturing evidence.

## Finite separate-product engineering and certification plan

**Product boundary:** hypothetical desk-scale four-tool writer with a
single XY carriage and linear tool rack. It is a *separate future product*,
not an implicit Atrament desktop release feature. No machine was built,
connected or certified. The following are sequential design gates; each
requires independent practical evidence before physical operator use.

| Gate | Required artifact | Acceptance or refusal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Electronics | Power tree, motor limits, wiring map, protected interlocks | Refuse unknown current/thermal or unprotected fault paths |
| 2. Firmware | Versioned command protocol, watchdog, bounded stops and status | Refuse commands with unknown version or unobserved position |
| 3. Mechanics/enclosure | Guarded pinch points, safe park, rack and clamp envelope | Refuse reachable moving hazard or unguarded pen/clip strike |
| 4. Independent stop | Physical emergency-stop and power-fault isolation design | Refuse any software-only claim of effective emergency stop |
| 5. Registration | Four tool-offset records, page corners and clearance maps | Refuse unknown orientation, measured error or partial tool pickup |
| 6. Endurance | Repeat docking, long-page cycles, noise/thermal/ink logs | Refuse unmeasured wear, drift, spills or tip collisions |
| 7. Manufacturing | Traceable bill of materials, calibration jig and service plan | Refuse untraceable revisions or service without protective procedure |
| 8. Legal/product release | Region-specific safety/EMC review by competent experts | Refuse sale or certification claim absent applicable evidence |

**Finite theoretical bench sequence:** with power isolated, first inspect
fused supply and cable-routing diagrams; validate a simulated disconnect
from firmware protocol state; review a static enclosure drawing for tool
rack clearance; draft a no-motion page registration using four fiducials;
then define 100 repeated hypothetical dock/undock cycles per slot and the
fault logs needed to evaluate wear. The number 100 is a future test-plan
sample count, **not** cycles executed or a claimed endurance guarantee.
Before any eventual live test, define stop conditions, competent operator,
protective barrier, measured tool clearance and independent power isolation.

**Worked refusal:** a partial tool pickup after brownout leaves slot 2
reported but the carriage position unknown. Even if the firmware reconnects,
reject further tool-change, pen-down or home commands until an authorized
person isolates power, inspects the machine, restores known mechanical state,
recalibrates and reissues a plan. Firmware restart is not recovery evidence.

**Cross-product interface:** desktop Atrament may inspect a typed immutable
plan; only a future independently reviewed physical adapter can arm or send
commands to this custom machine. Its firmware identity, enclosure state,
operator training and test ledger may never be fabricated from simulated
backend domain records. Target noise, vibration and desk dimensions require
physical measurements with instruments and declared uncertainty.

**Assumptions and risks:** no schematic, firmware, PCB, enclosure, test
fixture, working emergency stop, EMC evaluation, accredited testing or
manufacturing evidence was produced. The proposed plan does not establish
compliance with any particular law or technical standard and authorizes no
assembly, powered test, product shipment or user operation.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical finite engineering/certification roadmap, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** eight sequential evidence gates, 100-cycle future study,
  power-loss refusal and separation from the single-pen software product.
- **Unavailable:** built custom device, measured hardware behavior,
  certification or physical operation authorization.
